# Copy this file to `.env` (or `.env.local`). `pnpm setup:local` does that copy
# for you, from this file and from nothing else — plus one value it GENERATES
# rather than copies (IMPERSONATION_SECRET; see its block below for why).
# `.env*` is gitignored — never commit real secrets.
#
# THE VALUES BELOW ARE A WORKING LOCAL-EMULATOR CONFIG, not blanks to fill in.
# That is deliberate: a template of empty strings makes the first run fail on the
# env fail-fast, and the fix — inventing plausible Firebase values — is exactly the
# step a newcomer has no way to get right. Everything preset here points at
# `pnpm emulators` and the compose SeaweedFS, and none of it is a credential: the
# emulators verify no key and the S3 identity is the one in `seaweedfs.s3.json`,
# which only ever guards a local container.
#
# THE RULE THAT GOVERNS WHAT MAY BE PRESET: a value belongs here only if a copy of
# this file carrying it cannot be a security hole anywhere. A placeholder that
# merely looks fake is not enough — what matters is whether it VALIDATES, because
# a value that passes its schema works, and something that works is never
# replaced. So nothing whose whole job is to be secret carries a value here, and
# each of the three is shipped in the weakest state that still teaches:
#
#   IMPERSONATION_SECRET  present but EMPTY, and its capability has no toggle —
#                         so a `.env` made by COPYING this file fails the boot
#                         check by name until you generate one. `pnpm setup:local`
#                         does not copy that line, it GENERATES the value, which
#                         is why the supported path never meets that refusal and
#                         a hand copy always does.
#   FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL present but EMPTY, and deliberately NOT boot-checked
#   FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY  while an emulator host is set: lib/env/registry.ts marks
#                         that group `enabled: () => !isEmulatorTarget()`. It
#                         starts failing by name the moment you comment the
#                         emulator hosts out — which is exactly when you need it.
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY     COMMENTED OUT, alongside a commented AI_ENABLED. The
#                         capability is off, so this is never validated at all;
#                         turning AI_ENABLED on is what makes it boot-required.
#
# Only values that authenticate against nothing real are preset.
#
# EVERY PRESET VALUE MUST BE REPLACED FOR A REAL PROJECT. They are named to be
# obvious on sight (`demo-aisa`, `local-emulator-*`, `aisa-dev-*`) — nothing here
# will authenticate against anything real, so a deployment carrying them fails at
# its first Firebase call rather than quietly working with the wrong project. The
# two emulator-host blocks near the bottom are what make this file "local": comment
# them out and the app talks to real Firebase, which then needs real credentials.
#
# Public (NEXT_PUBLIC_*) vars are validated at BUILD time: next.config.ts calls
# validateClientEnv() from lib/env/client.ts. They are INLINED into the browser
# bundle, so a missing one has to fail the build — it cannot be fixed afterwards.
#
# Server capability config (S3, AI, impersonation) is validated at server BOOT:
# instrumentation-node.ts runs collectConfigFailures() over the capability
# manifest in lib/env/registry.ts, so a build that ships an ENABLED capability
# refuses to start without its secret — the check moved from first use to boot.
# Each capability owns its own schema next to its code (lib/storage/config.ts,
# lib/ai/config.ts, lib/auth/impersonation.config.ts).
#
# There is NO bypass flag. Validation always runs; a secret-less build supplies
# explicit non-secret dummy PUBLIC values instead (scripts/public-env-fixture.ts,
# used by `pnpm build:ci` and vitest), so a green build actually means something.
# The Containerfile deliberately runs plain `pnpm build` with the REAL public
# build args — the CI fixture must never reach a production bundle.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client (public) — Firebase web SDK config.
# Prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_ so Next.js inlines them into the browser bundle.
# These are not secret, but they ARE environment-specific.
#
# The presets are the shape the web SDK insists on (it validates the SHAPE, never
# the values, and the auth emulator checks neither). `demo-aisa` matches the
# `--project demo-aisa` that `pnpm emulators` runs under; the emulator suite
# treats a `demo-` prefix as "no real project exists behind this".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=local-emulator-api-key
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=demo-aisa.firebaseapp.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=demo-aisa
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=demo-aisa.appspot.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=000000000000
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:000000000000:web:0000000000000000000000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server (secret) — Firebase Admin service-account credentials.
# Never expose these to the client. Get them from a service-account JSON key.
#
# LEFT EMPTY ON PURPOSE, and the app still boots: `lib/env/registry.ts` marks the
# Admin credential `enabled: () => !isEmulatorTarget()`, so pointing at an emulator
# genuinely does not require a service account. Fill these in at the same time you
# comment out the emulator hosts below — not before.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=demo-aisa
FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL=
# Paste the full PEM. Wrap in double quotes; literal `\n` is normalized to real
# newlines at read time, e.g. "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server (secret) — S3-compatible object storage.
#
# GENERIC on purpose: nothing in the code names a backend, so switching is an
# env change only. The default is self-hosted SeaweedFS; Cloudflare R2 is the
# managed alternative. Requests are always path-style.
#
# The presets are the compose service (`docker compose up -d seaweedfs`, which is
# what `pnpm setup:local` starts) and the identity declared in `seaweedfs.s3.json`
# — one file owns those credentials, this just points at them. Do NOT run
# SeaweedFS without `-s3.config`: started that way it accepts EVERY request,
# signed or not, so signature and permission bugs pass locally and only surface
# against a real backend.
#
#   R2 : S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#        S3_REGION=auto  (R2 requires exactly this)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8333
S3_BUCKET=aisa-dev
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=aisa-dev-key
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=aisa-dev-secret
# Optional — where the BROWSER reaches storage, when that is not where the SERVER
# does. Defaults to S3_ENDPOINT, which is correct here: on the host dev path one
# address serves both. Set it wherever they differ — Compose already does
# (`compose.env`), and so does any deployment whose app reaches storage over a
# private hostname or VPC endpoint while the reader needs a public one. A
# presigned URL is signed against a host, so the endpoint that mints it is the
# one baked in; there is no rewriting it afterwards.
# S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=https://storage.example.com
# Optional — defaults to us-east-1. SigV4 needs a region even where the backend
# ignores it (SeaweedFS accepts any); R2 mandates `auto`.
# S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server — AI / inference. OFF BY DEFAULT.
#
# AI_ENABLED states INTENT separately from the credential, and that is the whole
# design: with "a key being present means on", a forgotten or typo'd key silently
# disables inference and nothing can tell "off on purpose" from "broken".
#
#   unset / false / 0 : off. No key is read, the provider is never initialized,
#                       the AI nav entry is hidden, /ai redirects to /403, and the
#                       action refuses. A fresh clone runs with no Anthropic account.
#   true / 1          : on. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY becomes REQUIRED at boot.
#
# Strict enum, like LOG_LEVEL: `AI_ENABLED=yes` is REJECTED at boot, never read
# as off — config that lies is worse than config that fails.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY matters only while AI_ENABLED is on; then the app refuses to
# boot without it, naming the variable, rather than 500-ing on first use. One
# provider, hardcoded: there is no provider seam and no offline stub, so "on"
# means a real key. @ai-sdk/anthropic reads this variable itself, so the name is
# fixed, not chosen. To drop the capability entirely, `trash lib/ai/` and remove
# its line from lib/env/registry.ts and lib/auth/capability-availability.ts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# AI_ENABLED=false
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server (secret) — admin impersonation.
#
# Signs the overlay ticket that lets an admin act as another user; the admin's
# own login is never replaced. At least 32 characters — the secret's whole value
# is its entropy. Boot-required while the feature ships, like the keys above.
#
# EMPTY ON PURPOSE — and for a different reason than the other empty values here.
# FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL / FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY are empty because they are simply
# ABSENT: there is no local value for them, and against an emulator none is
# wanted. This one is empty because any value shipped here would WORK. It is the
# signing key for an AUTH BYPASS, and a placeholder long enough to pass `min(32)` in
# lib/auth/impersonation.config.ts would VALIDATE — every clone would share one
# publicly-known key, and it would work, so nothing would ever tell you to change
# it. Empty, the boot check in instrumentation-node.ts refuses to serve and names
# this variable. That refusal is the only thing that reliably teaches.
#
# Generate one, per environment, and never reuse it:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# `pnpm setup:local` does that for you when it CREATES `.env` (fresh randomness
# on your machine, not a value copied from here). A bare `cp .env.example .env`
# deliberately does not — it leaves this empty and the app tells you why.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPERSONATION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client (optional) — error monitoring.
#
# A DSN is public by design: it ships in every browser bundle, so one key serves
# client, server and edge alike and there is no secret twin to keep in sync.
# Leave unset and the SDK no-ops — the boilerplate must build without a Sentry
# account. Points at any Sentry-DSN-compatible backend (GlitchTip is the default).
#
# What is sent: an exception, its stack, the route, `release` and `environment`.
# No email, uid, cookie, header, request body or query string — see the allow-list
# in lib/observability/sentry.ts. Tracing is OFF; a project opts in deliberately.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<host>/<project-id>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server — local emulators. COMMENT BOTH OUT to use live Firebase.
#
# These two are what make this file local. They switch the Admin SDK's target,
# they are what `isEmulatorTarget()` reads to stop requiring a service account,
# and they are the fence `pnpm seed` / `pnpm db:reset` refuse to run without —
# which is why those commands provably cannot reach a real database.
# The ports come from `firebase.json`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:8080
FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client — point the web SDK at local emulators (browser only). NEXT_PUBLIC_ so
# they inline into the bundle. COMMENT BOTH OUT to use live Firebase.
#
# Separate from the server pair above because they are read in a different place
# at a different time: these are inlined by `next build` for the browser, those are
# read by the server at run time. Both halves have to move together — a browser
# still pointed at real Firebase while the server talks to the emulator signs you
# in against an account the server cannot see.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:8080
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099

# Structured-log threshold. debug | info | warn | error. Defaults to `info`;
# a value outside the enum throws on boot rather than silently falling back.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Build stamp, reported by /api/health. Populated by the Containerfile at
# image build; both default to `unknown`, which is the honest answer under
# `pnpm dev` — there is no build to stamp. Never set these by hand.
# BUILD_SHA=
# BUILT_AT=

# The same commit, in the form the BROWSER can see: it becomes the `release` on
# every error-monitoring event, client and server. A separate key only because a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is the only thing `next build` inlines into a client bundle —
# the Containerfile derives it from the same `--build-arg BUILD_SHA`, so there is
# nothing to keep in sync by hand. Also defaults to `unknown`.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_SHA=
